[[Azerbaijani cuisine]] includes a variety of '''soups and stews'''. 

== Azerbaijani soups and stews ==
[[File:Dovga e-citizen.JPG|thumb|400x400px|Dovga]]
[[File:Azerbaijani dish piti.JPG|thumb|456x456px|Piti]]
* '''Bozartma (lamb stew)'''  is a Caucasian soup with lamb popular in [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]] and [[Azerbaijan]]. The word ''bozartma''  is derived from the [[Turkish language|Turkish]] word ''bozartmaq'' which means 'cooked chunk of meat'. Lamb shank needs to be steamed for a long time before it is fully cooked.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://flavorsofbaku.com/portfolio-view/bozartma-lamb-stew/|title=Bozartma (Lamb Stew) - Flavors of Baku|work=Flavors of Baku|access-date=2018-02-28|language=en-US}}</ref>
*'''[[Dovga]]  '''is a soup made of plain yoghurt and a variety of herbs. The soup can be served either cold or warm. Different herbs may be used for cooking ''dovga'' – coriander, dill, mint, [[chervil]], parsley, etc.
* '''[[Joshpara|Dushbara]]''' is a soup made of very small [[dumplings]] with ground meat inside which are boiled in lamb broth. ''Dushbara'' is usually served with vinegar.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://flavorsofbaku.com/portfolio-view/dushbara/|title=Dushbara - Flavors of Baku|work=Flavors of Baku|access-date=2018-02-28|language=en-US}}</ref>
* '''[[Khash (dish)|Khash]]''' is cow's-foot tendon stew or soup of [[Armenians|Armenian]] origin.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Adjarian |first=Hrachia |title=Armenian Etymological Dictionary |year=1973 |isbn=9783447036405 |pages=346}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Heratsi |first=Mkhitar |title=Relief Of Fevers |year=1178 |isbn=978-0-8134-3032-4 |pages=Chapter 6 and Chapter 10}}</ref> It is a popular dish in [[Azerbaijan]] eaten on weekend mornings during winter. ''Khash'' has to be cooked for at least 8 hours, until the broth becomes very thick. Cow's stomach and head may also be used for cooking ''khash''.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://flavorsofbaku.com/portfolio-view/khash/|title=Khash (tendon stew) - Flavors of Baku|work=Flavors of Baku|access-date=2018-02-28|language=en-US}}</ref>
* '''Kyufta [[bozbash]],''' the Azerbaijani dish, usually eaten during wintertime. It is made of ground beef or lamb formed into medium-sized balls. The word ''kyufta'' originates from the Persian word  ''kuftan'', which means 'to beat', because the meat for ''kyufta'' needs to be beaten before being formed into balls. The word ''bozbash'' comes from Turkish-Azerbaijani words ''boz'', which is translated as 'gray', and ''bash'' which is translated as 'head'. It is served with a variety of vegetables.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://flavorsofbaku.com/portfolio-view/kufta-bozbash/|title=Kufta-Bozbash - Flavors of Baku|work=Flavors of Baku|access-date=2018-02-28|language=en-US}}</ref>
* '''[[Doghramaj|Ovdukh]]''' is cold yoghurt and herb soup. It contains cucumber and herbs. Hard-boiled eggs and minced meat can sometimes be added.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://news.az/recipes/24783|title=News.Az -   Ovdukh - Cold yoghurt and herb soup|website=news.az|access-date=2018-02-28}}</ref> 
* '''[[Piti (food)|Piti]]''' is a dish popular in the [[Shaki, Azerbaijan|Sheki]] region of Azerbaijan. ''Piti'' is cooked in ceramic clay pots called. It is traditionally made of chickpeas, lamb, onion, saffron, chestnut, dried plum and sheep tail fat. ''Piti'' should be eaten in two steps. First comes the soup with bread, then the remaining part. 

